#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128815 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f8fa2b3-ccaa-5390-89fa-03e338cf8690" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Amplitude of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/id #uuid "e2e5002a-d3ae-5831-b405-41618960aee4" :question/math-academy-id "e-15609" :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x) = -2\\sin\\left(x-\\dfrac\\pi 3\\right) + 1$?" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)= A\\sin(Bx +C) + D\n$$\n\nthe amplitude is $\\mid A \\mid$.\n\nIn our case, we have that $A=-2$. Therefore, the amplitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid A &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128816 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1eeb42af-74ee-53d7-af9f-be171f77968f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/id #uuid "7f6626c7-c3d3-5f4e-b2c7-1e9bf07e883c" :question/math-academy-id "e-15610" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $y=\\cos(2x)$." :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form\n\n$$\ny = A\\cos(Bx +C) + D\n$$\n\nthe period is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{B}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=2$. Therefore, the period is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\left(2\\right)}=\\pi\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128817 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5696c2d5-4e35-5ed3-a4a2-6fe9b158e461" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/id #uuid "61b6821f-3ee5-5dd8-b8a3-ee1859645237" :question/math-academy-id "e-15611" :question/problem "What is the range of the function $f(x) = -4\\cos(3x)-2$?" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\cos x$ is\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\cos x \\leq 1\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\leq\\cos\\left(3x \\right) \\leq 1\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-4$ and then subtracting $2$, we get\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}- 1 & \\le  \\cos   ( 3 x ) \\le  1 \\\\ 4 & \\ge  - 4 \\cos   ( 3 x ) \\ge  - 4 \\\\ 2 & \\ge  - 4 \\cos   ( 3 x ) - 2 \\ge  - 6 \\\\ - 6 & \\le  f ( x ) \\le  2.\\end{array}$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $f(x)\\in [-6,2]$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128818 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "933b20c7-323f-5443-9cf4-97e87fa2c6fc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Midline of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/id #uuid "bfa446cd-c215-59c2-b84d-35d7897e9e34" :question/math-academy-id "e-15615" :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $-6$ and a maximum value of $8$." :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half.\n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{y_{\\textrm{min}}+y_{\\textrm{max}}}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $y_{\\textrm{min}}$ and $y_{\\textrm{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{y_{\\min} + y_{\\max}}{2} &= \\frac{-6 + 8}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5696c2d5-4e35-5ed3-a4a2-6fe9b158e461" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0f7c438-abaa-58c9-8bc8-561dd28fc314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in [-4,0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251341} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4e5b53-a634-5d6d-8c51-2e0831d6f973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251342} {:answer/id #uuid "78aa5a40-09d2-5735-a097-9f636e0fd13d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in [-4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251343} {:answer/id #uuid "db7d3f02-9ef0-5ffb-9d52-29c193f3485b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in (-4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251344} {:answer/id #uuid "fcff4627-0b78-5a22-a713-c1dd3504169e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in (-4,0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0f7c438-abaa-58c9-8bc8-561dd28fc314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in [-4,0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251341} :answer-field/id #uuid "af264a72-7d28-58e0-bdfc-f3f91226bcba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251340}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b211722-74ca-5ce0-9e29-ed681235d5e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-10379" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-2.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $2$ and then subtracting $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\le 1 \\\\  & -2\\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\le 2 \\\\  & -4\\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-2\\le 0 \\\\  & -4\\le y\\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $y\\in [-4,0].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251346 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f8fa2b3-ccaa-5390-89fa-03e338cf8690" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Amplitude of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3880f5eb-4e0a-5318-bc61-488aeceed57a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251348} {:answer/id #uuid "62e8bef9-0992-570e-aaca-1ce51d2bf9f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251349} {:answer/id #uuid "8a255fc0-7ce6-523f-8af2-7b57ce0d7d78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251350} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce92272-95ab-53e4-b3bc-373982c51258" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251351} {:answer/id #uuid "59966a48-82ee-529d-863c-592ed5c8abb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a255fc0-7ce6-523f-8af2-7b57ce0d7d78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251350} :answer-field/id #uuid "009e4def-0c94-5ff5-b42f-5e7c649d1b77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251347}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "662c0bc2-f0ef-5c70-b92b-c197c2591fb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-117569" :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{1}{4})?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$ \n\nSince $A=1,$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|1|=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f8fa2b3-ccaa-5390-89fa-03e338cf8690" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Amplitude of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4a7bf46-d044-51e9-a154-9442e8a92916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251355} {:answer/id #uuid "ae483747-c569-58dd-a494-b3b8ae03018e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251356} {:answer/id #uuid "610cf396-50b8-5706-938b-beacca897a5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251357} {:answer/id #uuid "c8db415e-fbcb-5ef5-b05c-5dc1a95cfa77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251358} {:answer/id #uuid "bc5f3d9c-c57c-5fd4-b964-2ca2a4554098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc5f3d9c-c57c-5fd4-b964-2ca2a4554098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251359} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb51527e-9aed-5787-bbbb-cfc71394113f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251354}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "659b9cea-9276-552a-b2fc-bba13b13abcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-117570" :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+3)?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nSince $A=4,$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|4|=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f8fa2b3-ccaa-5390-89fa-03e338cf8690" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Amplitude of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c2f673f-6248-5434-b3db-21539824bd3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251362} {:answer/id #uuid "c6990aba-a235-5fe4-8a60-34f74c3c6e1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251363} {:answer/id #uuid "e65db530-2a68-5fc3-9c59-dfbbf4dedb9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251364} {:answer/id #uuid "6206da6e-fb36-5500-b80d-972bc2bfc2eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251365} {:answer/id #uuid "c5806aa9-c084-5d93-9954-ceea4031c4be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6990aba-a235-5fe4-8a60-34f74c3c6e1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251363} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8db0155-0bd3-5c0f-9a60-ffcf3409dbac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251361}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b13d28d7-880d-5827-bd34-75df64c54f17" :question/math-academy-id "q-117574" :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+1)?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nSince $A=-7,$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|-7|=7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f8fa2b3-ccaa-5390-89fa-03e338cf8690" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Amplitude of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ac56eae-c05b-58de-acc5-d7f3a6c96076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251369} {:answer/id #uuid "61249cad-427f-57f4-a2bd-0b80e95eda17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251370} {:answer/id #uuid "046933a8-bb5e-51ff-94b6-281d86aa8e10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251371} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe5fd2b-4758-51d0-ac78-35e1c905bc5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251372} {:answer/id #uuid "7a623a08-63bb-595e-b884-f265e99d0857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ac56eae-c05b-58de-acc5-d7f3a6c96076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251369} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c045123-8cf8-5972-843a-35e86c3d6964" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251368}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c292691-d76c-561e-a9a3-3b6c75eed4d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-117576" :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{5}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite the function as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{5}=-\\frac{9}{5}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $A=-\\frac{9}{5}.$ Therefore, the amplitude is $|A|=|-\\frac{9}{5}|=\\frac{9}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1eeb42af-74ee-53d7-af9f-be171f77968f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c1dfa1d-a605-505d-954f-55c969b43e8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251376} {:answer/id #uuid "86c4ea6d-e778-5147-b083-17ce4fff42b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251377} {:answer/id #uuid "500bec3f-1365-5e4f-b7fd-def30e7407a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251378} {:answer/id #uuid "e511886f-610c-597e-9230-becda2e8f0d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251379} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c695ae-9f38-597d-9693-2deaf7327c8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1c695ae-9f38-597d-9693-2deaf7327c8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251380} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d6050af-b75b-53b3-ab73-e3066146e0f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251375}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94a800c1-7f3e-54d4-bc38-628730349bda" :question/math-academy-id "q-117578" :question/problem "What is the period of the function $f(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{3}{4}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{(\\frac{3}{4})}=\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1eeb42af-74ee-53d7-af9f-be171f77968f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d78f8899-2690-5907-acc5-54b9412d91af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251383} {:answer/id #uuid "b469637b-ea16-5232-ba83-a4a15079284a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{480}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251384} {:answer/id #uuid "1178da14-cb8f-5854-9883-33bfa07b97ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{300}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251385} {:answer/id #uuid "a22788de-027d-5e83-b54b-46d7b75a5be6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{270}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251386} {:answer/id #uuid "6bbafeca-d183-5023-8a89-f30dd6104f66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{120}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a22788de-027d-5e83-b54b-46d7b75a5be6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{270}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251386} :answer-field/id #uuid "73b99e5f-0946-5698-be9b-0c5670bb6590" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251382}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "810918b3-6c3d-59d4-9b10-f0eca91e979e" :question/math-academy-id "q-117579" :question/problem "What is the period of the function $f(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4x}{3}-{45}^{∘})?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{4}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{(\\frac{4}{3})}={270}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "933b20c7-323f-5443-9cf4-97e87fa2c6fc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Midline of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84ff420b-cd3a-598b-be07-d84c30fdc42f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251390} {:answer/id #uuid "f6341528-c7b6-5399-8b52-720c78550729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251391} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a7334e-2881-537b-973e-09c8ab166460" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251392} {:answer/id #uuid "05f916aa-39c6-5a56-b4df-c5a7f6b98b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251393} {:answer/id #uuid "a5cfdfd8-f2fb-5251-9ec2-7ccd0b9554cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84ff420b-cd3a-598b-be07-d84c30fdc42f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251390} :answer-field/id #uuid "71e63e7f-f2ea-5901-8d3e-c21ff4233228" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251389}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7322d6d2-2ef7-599c-98ef-07bc2b13a18e" :question/math-academy-id "q-236890" :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $2$ and a maximum value of $10.$" :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{\\text{min}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{2+10}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "933b20c7-323f-5443-9cf4-97e87fa2c6fc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Midline of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5edb333-d847-5de9-a3fc-d5e0f774c46b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251397} {:answer/id #uuid "4088a1a9-9438-5c09-b074-79a8c0e132e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251398} {:answer/id #uuid "58235a33-7803-59b5-964d-3008a3b7aa25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251399} {:answer/id #uuid "2512511b-ea5e-5dd0-ba35-0854399ec88b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251400} {:answer/id #uuid "476bf7f3-9f84-51e6-9bcb-821112526d80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "476bf7f3-9f84-51e6-9bcb-821112526d80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251401} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dede64d-8a4d-55e9-83f1-870b7ae0a1fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251396}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2669db28-68a5-5a9b-a378-c1a3991efc67" :question/math-academy-id "q-236893" :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $-7$ and a maximum value of $3.$" :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{\\text{min}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{-7+3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "933b20c7-323f-5443-9cf4-97e87fa2c6fc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Midline of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7123c4f-eb2f-5b18-b878-c2c2dc5dda4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :a